From: lagz0ne <15101444+lagz0ne@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 08:11:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 36/52] perf(canvas): culled frames drawn whole on one WebGL2
 canvas, no DOM for them (experiment, doop:gpu)

Every FrameShell is its own compositor layer, and Layerize and Commit run per
layer on every camera frame, so a page of ~190 frames at fit pays ~150 ms of
Layerize over a 30-event wheel pan. Making the shells cheaper does not help;
only removing them does. With localStorage 'doop:gpu' = 'on' (or 'force', which
also accepts a software-rendered context) a culled frame whose preview texture is
ready has nothing in the render tree: no shell, no name, no hit target.
Unset, StagePictures renders nothing and FrameSlot keeps today's tree.

What is drawn (gpuPictures.ts, StagePictures.tsx):
- The picture: one textured quad per frame, rotation included, under the
  world. Paint order comes from a depth buffer (item i of n at depth
  1 - 2(i+1)/(n+1), LEQUAL), so stacking matches frames.z.
- The name (gpuLabels.ts): every distinct name rendered once into a 2D-canvas
  atlas at the screen's density, in the font, weight, letter spacing and
  colour read off a hidden probe styled like the shell's title, so it follows
  the theme and the web font. It keeps 12px on screen at every zoom, LABEL_GAP
  6 above the frame, and is cut with an ellipsis at the frame's on-screen width
  by per-grapheme prefix widths measured once (text-overflow's rule). One
  instanced draw, rebuilt only when the scene, atlas or settled zoom changes;
  the origin snaps to device px when upright.
- The hover ring (brand at 55%, 1 screen px), on the canvas.

Which frames: a frame is drawn only when it is a candidate (has HTML, not a
canvas element, not hidden, locked, approved/under review, synced or GitHub:
those carry icons or a badge and stay shells), its texture is ready, and
nothing still in the DOM lies under it or under its name strip, since the
canvas sits under the world. Selected, live, woken and streaming frames are
the DOM. A shell under a drawn frame cascades: it keeps the frames over it in
the DOM too. The set is recomputed at rest, never per camera frame.

Pointer: Stage's onDown asks gpuFrameAt when no shell is hit: arithmetic over
the drawn list back to front (rotation, the name strip), then the same wake
and press re-delivery a shell gets. Hover is pointermove arithmetic against a
cached stage origin (refreshed on resize and press), so it forces no layout.

Fallbacks: context loss hands every frame back to its shell; restore draws
them again once textures reload. A frame whose texture is not ready, or whose
name does not fit the atlas, stays a shell.

Camera cost: setCamera writes uniforms and draws; no measuring, allocation or
React render per frame (~0.4 ms of rAF script for 188 quads, swiftshader).

Measured on a 188-frame page, headless Chromium with GPU compositing
(swiftshader), wheel pan of 30 events, off/force interleaved, two rounds:
- layers at fit: 204 -> 9 (shells 188 -> 0, world elements 1130 -> 2);
  at 60%: 134 -> 31 (shells 182 -> 0)
- fit: main thread 270-337 -> 128-216 ms, Layerize 136-164 -> 10-18 ms,
  compositor 196-246 -> 97-120 ms (load 5.2-5.5)
- 60%: main thread 426-627 -> 236-312 ms, Layerize 188-268 -> 38-46 ms,
  compositor 266-374 -> 130-173 ms (load 4.9-15.9)
- names at fit vs the DOM title, 88 boxes: mean |diff| 0.8/255 light,
  2.5/255 dark; pixels off by more than 32: 0.06% light, 3.3% dark (glyph
  antialiasing; same cut, position and stacking)

Not done: kerning across the ellipsis cut; DPR > 1 and a hardware GPU are
untested; picture draws are one call each (instancing needs a texture array).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
